var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"expectations/#expectations","page":"Expectations","text":"Inference algorithms often need the expectation mathbbE_qf of a function f under a distribution q. BayesBase defines a protocol for computing it, BayesBase.expectation(backend, f, q), where the backend argument selects the method: a closed form, a quadrature rule or a sampler, for example. Backends are defined in other packages, such as ExpectationApproximations.jl. An algorithm written against the protocol works with every backend that supports its distributions, and the user of the algorithm chooses the method.\n\nThe design is similar to DifferentiationInterface.jl, which has a backend argument that selects the differentiation package and prepare_* functions whose result is reused by later calls. Here, BayesBase.prepare_expectation(backend, q) does the work that depends only on q, such as computing quadrature nodes and weights, and its result is reused for many integrands.\n\nThe names of the protocol are public but not exported. Call them qualified, as BayesBase.expectation.\n\nusing BayesBase, Distributions\n\nbackend = SomeBackend()                               # from a backend package\nBayesBase.expectation(backend, x -> x^2, Normal(0.0, 1.0))\n\nprepared = BayesBase.prepare_expectation(backend, Normal(0.0, 1.0))\nBayesBase.expectation(prepared, x -> x^2)             # many integrands, nodes computed once","title":"Expectations"},{"category":"section","location":"expectations/#Computing-expectations","page":"Expectations","text":"expectation(backend, f, q) returns the expectation, or throws a BayesBase.UnsupportedExpectationError when backend cannot compute it. For several integrands under the same q, call prepare_expectation(backend, q) once and then expectation(prepared, f) for each integrand.\n\nA backend implements one of two things for the distributions it supports. A closed form is a method of expectation for specific types of f and q, such as expectation(::MyBackend, ::typeof(log), q::Gamma). A preparation is a method of prepare_expectation for specific types of q. It returns an object with its own method expectation(prepared, f), which works for any integrand, including integrands whose values are dual numbers. Most numerical backends return a BayesBase.ExpectationPointSet, a weighted sum over points.\n\nBackends define these methods for specific families of distributions, not for all q. Products of distributions and routers rely on this.","title":"Computing expectations"},{"category":"section","location":"expectations/#What-a-backend-supports","page":"Expectations","text":"A backend declares the pairs it supports with methods of BayesBase.supports_expectation. These methods take the types of f and q, so the compiler knows the answer. The integrand type Any means every integrand, which is what a numerical method declares:\n\nBayesBase.supports_expectation(::MyQuadrature, ::Type, ::Type{<:Normal}) = true        # any f\nBayesBase.supports_expectation(::MyClosedForms, ::Type{typeof(log)}, ::Type{<:Gamma}) = true\n\nFor the expectation of a log-density, E_q[log p(x)], the integrand is Base.Fix1(logpdf, p), which is Julia's callable for x -> logpdf(p, x). A backend with a closed form declares and implements it by dispatch on that type:\n\nBayesBase.supports_expectation(::MyClosedForms, ::Type{<:Base.Fix1{typeof(logpdf), <:Normal}}, ::Type{<:Normal}) = true\nBayesBase.expectation(::MyClosedForms, f::Base.Fix1{typeof(logpdf), <:Normal}, q::Normal) = …\n\nA backend with many closed forms writes each one together with its declaration through BayesBase.@supports_expectation. The macro defines the method and the method of supports_expectation for the same argument types, so a closed form cannot be added without being declared. The method always extends BayesBase.expectation, also when it is written expectation in a module that has not imported that name:\n\nimport BayesBase: @supports_expectation\n\n@supports_expectation function BayesBase.expectation(::MyClosedForms, ::typeof(log), q::Gamma)\n    α, θ = params(q)\n    return digamma(α) + log(θ)\nend\n\nSupport that depends on more than the types of one method is still declared with a method of supports_expectation, written out.","title":"What a backend supports"},{"category":"section","location":"expectations/#Gradients-of-expectations","page":"Expectations","text":"BayesBase.expectation_gradient(backend, f, q) is the gradient of mathbbE_q_thetaf with respect to the parameters theta of q. Natural-gradient and score-function methods, for example, need it. The parametrization is the one the type of q defines: params(q) for a distribution of Distributions.jl, so (μ, σ) for a Normal, and the natural parameters for an ExponentialFamilyDistribution of ExponentialFamily.jl.\n\nThe gradient is itself an expectation. When f and the support of q do not depend on theta,\n\nnabla_theta mathbbE_q_thetaf = mathbbE_qleftf  nabla_theta log qright\n\nso expectation_gradient(backend, f, q) computes the expectation of the integrand BayesBase.ExpectationScoreProduct(f, q), which is x -> f(x) * expectation_score(q, x). Everything above therefore applies to gradients unchanged:\n\nA backend with a closed form for a gradient defines a method of expectation for the type of that integrand, most simply with BayesBase.@supports_expectation_gradient, and declares its support the same way.\nA numerical backend that supports every integrand under q computes the gradient as the sum of f(x) * expectation_score(q, x) over its points. It needs BayesBase.expectation_score for the type of q, so it supports gradients only where BayesBase.has_expectation_score holds. BayesBase has the score of the common univariate families and of products of them. A package that adds a score adds a method of has_expectation_score too.\nA router chooses a closed form for the gradient when one of its backends has it, and a numerical backend otherwise, wherever they are in its list. It passes over a numerical backend for a family without a score. Labels, preparations and products work as for expectations, and with a preparation for q the gradient is expectation(prepared, ExpectationScoreProduct(f, q)).\nThe integrand f must return a scalar, which multiplies the score.\n\nimport BayesBase: @supports_expectation_gradient\n\n@supports_expectation_gradient function BayesBase.expectation_gradient(::MyClosedForms, ::typeof(log), q::Gamma)\n    α, θ = params(q)\n    return [trigamma(α), 1 / θ]  # with respect to the shape α and the scale θ\nend\n\nrouter = BayesBase.ExpectationFirstOf(MyClosedForms(), MyQuadrature())\nBayesBase.expectation_gradient(router, log, Gamma(2.0, 1.0))      # the closed form\nBayesBase.expectation_gradient(router, x -> x^2, Gamma(2.0, 1.0)) # quadrature of x² times the score","title":"Gradients of expectations"},{"category":"section","location":"expectations/#Labels","page":"Expectations","text":"A label is a property of a computation that a caller can rely on, such as BayesBase.ExpectationExact for a result that is exact up to rounding. A backend declares a label for all the pairs it supports or for some of them, and only where the property is guaranteed. Below, every closed form is exact, and the quadrature rule is exact for categorical distributions:\n\nBayesBase.has_expectation_label(::MyClosedForms, ::BayesBase.ExpectationExact) = true\nBayesBase.has_expectation_label(::MyQuadrature, ::Type, ::Type{<:Categorical}, ::BayesBase.ExpectationExact) = true","title":"Labels"},{"category":"section","location":"expectations/#Random-number-generators","page":"Expectations","text":"A stochastic backend stores its own random number generator, and expectation does not take one. The code that sets up the computation, such as an inference engine, gives the backend a generator with BayesBase.with_expectation_rng. A generator that the user has already set is never replaced.","title":"Random number generators"},{"category":"section","location":"expectations/#Products-of-distributions","page":"Expectations","text":"An expectation over several independent variables, which can be of different kinds (for example a categorical, a multivariate Gaussian and a point mass), is an expectation under a FactorizedJoint. The integrand receives a tuple with one value per component. A backend supports a product when it supports every component for any integrand, and a router when it supports every component, possibly each with a different backend. The expectation is computed as nested expectations over the preparations of the components, mathbbE_q_1x_1 mapsto mathbbE_q_2x_2 mapsto f((x_1 x_2)), so any preparation works, such as sigma points or a sampler. When every preparation is an BayesBase.ExpectationPointSet, the nesting is the product rule over the point sets, an BayesBase.ExpectationProductPointSet. An unsupported product throws an BayesBase.UnsupportedExpectationError. Labels are combined with BayesBase.combine_expectation_label: the product is exact only if every component is exact, and stochastic if any component is stochastic.","title":"Products of distributions"},{"category":"section","location":"expectations/#Routers","page":"Expectations","text":"A router is a backend that passes each expectation on to one of several other backends. BayesBase.ExpectationFirstOf chooses from the types of f and q alone, so the choice is made at compile time. BayesBase.ExpectationDynamicRouter chooses at run time among the backends that packages have registered, and is meant for interactive use. ExpectationFirstOf(ExpectationDynamicRouter()) turns the current choice of the dynamic router into an ExpectationFirstOf.","title":"Routers"},{"category":"function","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.expectation","page":"Expectations","text":"expectation(backend, f, q)\n\nThe expectation E_q[f] of the integrand f under the distribution q, computed by backend.\n\nA backend with a closed form adds a method for the specific types of f and q it covers. A numerical backend adds a method of prepare_expectation for the types of q it supports instead, and the fallback method computes expectation(prepare_expectation(backend, q), f). When the backend has neither, the fallback throws an UnsupportedExpectationError.\n\nexpectation(prepared, f)\n\nThe expectation of f from an object returned by prepare_expectation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.expectation"},{"category":"function","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.prepare_expectation","page":"Expectations","text":"prepare_expectation(backend, q)\n\nDo the work for expectations under q that does not depend on the integrand, such as computing nodes and weights, and return an object prepared. Then expectation(prepared, f) computes the expectation of any f, including integrands whose values are dual numbers.\n\nThe default returns a PreparedExpectation, which forwards every call to the backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.prepare_expectation"},{"category":"type","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.PreparedExpectation","page":"Expectations","text":"PreparedExpectation(backend, q)\n\nThe default result of prepare_expectation: no work is done in advance, and expectation(prepared, f) calls expectation(backend, f, q).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.PreparedExpectation"},{"category":"type","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.UnsupportedExpectationError","page":"Expectations","text":"UnsupportedExpectationError(backend, f, q)\n\nThrown when backend cannot compute the expectation of f under q.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.UnsupportedExpectationError"},{"category":"function","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.supports_expectation","page":"Expectations","text":"supports_expectation(backend, F::Type, Q::Type)\nsupports_expectation(backend, f, q)\n\nWhether backend computes expectations of integrands of type F under distributions of type Q. Backends add methods to the type form, which is false by default. F = Any asks whether every integrand is supported. A numerical method supports every integrand and declares it with supports_expectation(::MyBackend, ::Type, ::Type{<:MyFamily}) = true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.supports_expectation"},{"category":"function","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.which_expectation_backend","page":"Expectations","text":"which_expectation_backend(backend, F::Type, Q::Type)\nwhich_expectation_backend(backend, f, q)\n\nThe backend that computes the expectation, or nothing if there is none. A router returns the backend it chooses, or itself for a product of distributions. Any other backend returns itself if it supports the pair.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.which_expectation_backend"},{"category":"macro","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.@supports_expectation","page":"Expectations","text":"@supports_expectation function BayesBase.expectation(backend::B, f::F, q::Q) where {…}\n    …\nend\n\nDefine a closed form of BayesBase.expectation and declare that the backend supports it. Besides the method itself, the macro defines\n\nBayesBase.supports_expectation(::B, ::Type{<:F}, ::Type{<:Q}) where {…} = true\n\nwith the types and the where parameters of the definition. An argument without a type means Any. An integrand without a type therefore declares every integrand, as a numerical backend does, see BayesBase.supports_expectation. The definition can be in long or short form, and a docstring placed before the macro documents the method. Only methods of expectation with three positional arguments are accepted. Keyword arguments and default values are not. The method always extends BayesBase's function: a bare expectation is read as BayesBase.expectation, even where the name is not imported, and a function of another module is rejected.\n\nSupport that depends on more than the types of one method, such as \"wherever another backend has a closed form\", is declared with a method of supports_expectation written out instead.\n\nThe macro is public but not exported: write BayesBase.@supports_expectation, or import it with import BayesBase: @supports_expectation.\n\njulia> using Distributions\n\njulia> struct Moments end\n\njulia> BayesBase.@supports_expectation function BayesBase.expectation(::Moments, ::typeof(identity), q::Gamma)\n           α, θ = params(q)\n           return α * θ\n       end\n\njulia> BayesBase.supports_expectation(Moments(), identity, Gamma(2.0, 1.0))\ntrue\n\njulia> BayesBase.supports_expectation(Moments(), identity, Normal(0.0, 1.0))\nfalse\n\njulia> BayesBase.expectation(Moments(), identity, Gamma(2.0, 3.0))\n6.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.@supports_expectation"},{"category":"function","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.expectation_gradient","page":"Expectations","text":"expectation_gradient(backend, f, q)\n\nThe gradient of the expectation of f under q with respect to the parameters of q, nabla_theta mathbbE_q_thetaf, computed by backend. The parametrization is that of BayesBase.expectation_score: params(q) for a distribution of Distributions.jl, and the natural parameters for an ExponentialFamilyDistribution.\n\nThe gradient equals mathbbE_qf  nabla_theta log q, and it is computed as expectation(backend, ExpectationScoreProduct(f, q), q). Routers, labels and products therefore work for gradients as they do for expectations: a router chooses a backend with a closed form for the gradient when there is one, and otherwise a numerical backend that sums f(x) * expectation_score(q, x) over its points. Backends with closed forms define them with BayesBase.@supports_expectation_gradient.\n\nThe identity holds when f does not depend on θ and the support of q does not depend on θ, so it does not apply to the bounds of a Uniform, for example. The integrand f must return a scalar, which multiplies the score.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.expectation_gradient"},{"category":"function","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.supports_expectation_gradient","page":"Expectations","text":"supports_expectation_gradient(backend, F::Type, Q::Type)\nsupports_expectation_gradient(backend, f, q)\n\nWhether backend computes the gradient of the expectation of integrands of type F under distributions of type Q, see BayesBase.expectation_gradient. Backends declare support with methods of BayesBase.supports_expectation for the integrand ExpectationScoreProduct{F, Q}, not with methods of this function.\n\nA backend that supports every integrand under Q, such as a quadrature rule, computes gradients by summing f times the score, so it supports gradients only where BayesBase.has_expectation_score holds for Q. Routers apply the same rule, so they pass over such a backend for a family without a score and choose a backend with a closed form for the gradient instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.supports_expectation_gradient"},{"category":"type","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.ExpectationScoreProduct","page":"Expectations","text":"ExpectationScoreProduct(f, q)\n\nThe integrand x -> f(x) * expectation_score(q, x), whose expectation under q is the gradient of the expectation of f with respect to the parameters of q, see BayesBase.expectation_gradient. A backend with a closed form for the gradient adds a method of expectation for this type, for example with BayesBase.@supports_expectation_gradient. A numerical backend evaluates it at its points like any other integrand, which needs BayesBase.expectation_score for q.\n\nWith a preparation for q, the gradient is expectation(prepared, ExpectationScoreProduct(f, q)).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.ExpectationScoreProduct"},{"category":"function","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.expectation_score","page":"Expectations","text":"expectation_score(q, x)\n\nThe score of q at x: the gradient of log q(x; θ) with respect to the parameters θ of q. The parametrization is the one the type of q defines: params(q) for a distribution of Distributions.jl, and the natural parameters for an ExponentialFamilyDistribution of ExponentialFamily.jl. BayesBase has methods for Normal, LogNormal, Gamma, Exponential, Beta and a FactorizedJoint of those, whose score concatenates the scores of its components at the tuple x. Packages add methods for the types they define.\n\nThe default throws an ArgumentError. A backend with a closed form for the gradient does not need the score.\n\njulia> using Distributions\n\njulia> BayesBase.expectation_score(Normal(1.0, 2.0), 4.0)  # with respect to μ and σ\n2-element StaticArraysCore.SVector{2, Float64} with indices SOneTo(2):\n 0.75\n 0.625\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.expectation_score"},{"category":"function","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.has_expectation_score","page":"Expectations","text":"has_expectation_score(Q::Type)\nhas_expectation_score(q)\n\nWhether BayesBase.expectation_score has a method for distributions of type Q. It is false by default, true for the families with a score in BayesBase, and for a FactorizedJoint whose components all have one. A package that adds a method of expectation_score also adds a method of this function, which makes numerical backends compute gradients under its type, see BayesBase.supports_expectation_gradient.\n\njulia> using Distributions\n\njulia> BayesBase.has_expectation_score(Normal(0.0, 1.0))\ntrue\n\njulia> BayesBase.has_expectation_score(Categorical([0.5, 0.5]))\nfalse\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.has_expectation_score"},{"category":"macro","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.@supports_expectation_gradient","page":"Expectations","text":"@supports_expectation_gradient function BayesBase.expectation_gradient(backend::B, f::F, q::Q) where {…}\n    …\nend\n\nDefine a closed form of BayesBase.expectation_gradient and declare that the backend supports it. Besides the method itself, the macro defines the method that routers reach, which passes the integrand on to the closed form,\n\nBayesBase.expectation(backend::B, product::ExpectationScoreProduct{<:F, <:Q}, q::Q) where {…} =\n    BayesBase.expectation_gradient(backend, product.f, q)\n\nand the declaration\n\nBayesBase.supports_expectation(::B, ::Type{<:ExpectationScoreProduct{<:F, <:Q}}, ::Type{<:Q}) where {…} = true\n\nsee BayesBase.ExpectationScoreProduct. The result must be in the parametrization of BayesBase.expectation_score. Otherwise the macro works as BayesBase.@supports_expectation.\n\njulia> using Distributions\n\njulia> struct Moments end\n\njulia> BayesBase.@supports_expectation_gradient function BayesBase.expectation_gradient(::Moments, ::typeof(identity), q::Gamma)\n           α, θ = params(q)\n           return [θ, α]  # the gradient of α θ with respect to the shape α and the scale θ\n       end\n\njulia> BayesBase.supports_expectation_gradient(Moments(), identity, Gamma(2.0, 1.0))\ntrue\n\njulia> router = BayesBase.ExpectationFirstOf(Moments());\n\njulia> BayesBase.expectation_gradient(router, identity, Gamma(2.0, 3.0))\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 3.0\n 2.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.@supports_expectation_gradient"},{"category":"type","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.ExpectationLabel","page":"Expectations","text":"ExpectationLabel\n\nA property of the computation of an expectation that a caller can rely on. Labels are conservative: a backend declares a label only where it is guaranteed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.ExpectationLabel"},{"category":"type","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.ExpectationExact","page":"Expectations","text":"ExpectationExact()\n\nThe result equals the expectation up to rounding.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.ExpectationExact"},{"category":"type","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.ExpectationStochastic","page":"Expectations","text":"ExpectationStochastic()\n\nThe result depends on a random number generator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.ExpectationStochastic"},{"category":"function","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.has_expectation_label","page":"Expectations","text":"has_expectation_label(backend, label)\nhas_expectation_label(backend, F::Type, Q::Type, label)\nhas_expectation_label(backend, f, q, label)\n\nWhether backend has label. The first form asks about every pair the backend supports and is false by default. The other two forms ask about one pair. The type form falls back to the first form, and the instance form is also false when the backend does not support the pair.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.has_expectation_label"},{"category":"function","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.is_expectation_exact","page":"Expectations","text":"Whether backend computes the expectation of f under q exactly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.is_expectation_exact"},{"category":"function","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.is_expectation_stochastic","page":"Expectations","text":"Whether backend uses random numbers, for every pair or for the pair (f, q).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.is_expectation_stochastic"},{"category":"function","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.combine_expectation_label","page":"Expectations","text":"combine_expectation_label(label, a::Bool, b::Bool)\n\nWhether a computation made of two parts has label, given whether each part has it. The computation is exact if both parts are exact, and stochastic if either part is stochastic. A label defined in another package is combined like ExpectationExact, which is the conservative choice, unless that package adds a method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.combine_expectation_label"},{"category":"function","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.with_expectation_rng","page":"Expectations","text":"with_expectation_rng(backend, rng)\n\nbackend with rng as its random number generator if it has none yet. A backend that already has a generator keeps it, so a generator set by the user is never replaced. Composite backends, such as routers, pass rng on to their parts. The default returns backend unchanged.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.with_expectation_rng"},{"category":"function","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.get_expectation_rng","page":"Expectations","text":"get_expectation_rng(backend)\n\nThe random number generator of backend, or nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.get_expectation_rng"},{"category":"type","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.ExpectationPointSet","page":"Expectations","text":"ExpectationPointSet(points, weights)\n\nA prepared expectation that is a weighted sum, Σᵢ wᵢ f(xᵢ). Quadrature and cubature rules, sigma points, exact sums over a finite support and samples with equal weights all have this form. points and weights can be any collections indexed from one, including tuples. Product rules over a FactorizedJoint are built from the point sets of the components, see BayesBase.ExpectationProductPointSet.\n\nThe sum starts from its first term, so its type follows the values of the integrand. For example, an integrand that returns dual numbers needs no special handling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.ExpectationPointSet"},{"category":"type","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.ExpectationProductPointSet","page":"Expectations","text":"ExpectationProductPointSet(sets)\n\nThe product rule over a tuple of ExpectationPointSets: every combination of points, weighted by the product of their weights. The integrand receives a tuple of points. It is the preparation of a FactorizedJoint whose components all have a point set. Otherwise the preparation computes nested expectations over the components' preparations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.ExpectationProductPointSet"},{"category":"type","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.ExpectationFirstOf","page":"Expectations","text":"ExpectationFirstOf(backends...; prefer = (), exclude = ())\n\nA router: a backend that passes each expectation to the first of backends that supports the pair (f, q) and has none of the labels in exclude. If prefer is given, the first such backend that also has all the labels in prefer is chosen, and if there is none, the first such backend. prefer and exclude are a label or a tuple of labels. The choice depends only on the types of f and q, so type inference makes it and nothing is decided at run time.\n\nA product of distributions (FactorizedJoint) is routed component by component.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.ExpectationFirstOf"},{"category":"type","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.ExpectationDynamicRouter","page":"Expectations","text":"ExpectationDynamicRouter(registry = the global registry)\n\nA router that chooses among the registered backends at run time. Among the backends that support the pair, it prefers one that is exact for the pair, then one that is not stochastic, then one with a higher priority, and finally orders by the name of the backend's type. Backends with different names therefore do not depend on the order in which packages were loaded. Only backends that tie on all of these, such as two backends of the same type, keep the order in which they were registered.\n\nThe router is type-unstable. ExpectationFirstOf(ExpectationDynamicRouter()) freezes the current choice into a type-stable router, which is what an inference engine should use, as RxInfer's fallback schemes do by default. with_expectation_rng freezes it too, so that the generator reaches the backends.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.ExpectationDynamicRouter"},{"category":"type","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.ExpectationBackendRegistry","page":"Expectations","text":"ExpectationBackendRegistry()\n\nThe backends, each with a priority, among which an ExpectationDynamicRouter chooses.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.ExpectationBackendRegistry"},{"category":"function","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.register_expectation_backend!","page":"Expectations","text":"register_expectation_backend!(backend; priority = 0, registry = the global registry)\n\nMake backend available to ExpectationDynamicRouters. Registering a backend again changes its priority. Packages call this in __init__. A dynamic router cannot be registered, because it would choose among the backends that include itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.register_expectation_backend!"},{"category":"function","location":"expectations/#BayesBase.expectation_backend_priority","page":"Expectations","text":"expectation_backend_priority(backend; registry = the global registry) -> Union{Int, Nothing}\n\nThe priority backend was registered with, or nothing if it is not registered. A negative priority marks a last resort: a backend meant for the cases where no other applies. An inference report can use it to say where such a backend was used. An ExpectationFirstOf frozen from a dynamic router keeps the order that the priorities gave but not the priorities themselves, so this function looks them up in the registry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.expectation_backend_priority"},{"category":"section","location":"#BayesBase.jl","page":"Home","text":"BayesBase is a package that serves as an umbrella, defining, exporting, and re-exporting methods essential for Bayesian statistics specifically for the RxInfer ecosystem. \n\nRelated projects:\n\nRxInfer\nExponentialFamily","title":"BayesBase.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Index","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Index"},{"category":"section","location":"#library","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Library API"},{"category":"section","location":"#library-densities","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Generic densities"},{"category":"section","location":"#library-prod","page":"Home","text":"The prod function defines an interface to compute a product between two probability distributions over the same variable. It accepts a strategy as its first argument, which defines how the prod function should behave and what results you can expect.","title":"Product API"},{"category":"section","location":"#library-prod-strategies","page":"Home","text":"For certain distributions, it's possible to compute the product using a straightforward mathematical equation, yielding a closed-form solution.  However, for some distributions, finding a closed-form solution might not be feasible.  Various strategies ensure consistent behavior in these situations.  These strategies can either guarantee a fast and closed-form solution or, when necessary, fall back to a slower but more generic method.\n\nThese strategies offer flexibility and reliability when working with different types of distributions, ensuring that the package can handle a wide range of cases effectively.","title":"Product strategies"},{"category":"section","location":"#library-promotion-utilities","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Promotion type utilities"},{"category":"section","location":"#library-statsfuns","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Extra stats functions"},{"category":"section","location":"#matrix-structures","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Extra matrix structures"},{"category":"section","location":"#library-helpers","page":"Home","text":"","title":"Helper utilities"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.PointMass","page":"Home","text":"PointMass(point)\n\nA PointMass structure represents a delta distribution, a discrete probability distribution  where all probability mass is concentrated at a single point. This point is specified by  the provided point.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.PointMass"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.ContinuousUnivariateLogPdf","page":"Home","text":"ContinuousUnivariateLogPdf{ D <: DomainSets.Domain, F } <: AbstractContinuousGenericLogPdf\n\nGeneric continuous univariate distribution in a form of domain specification and logpdf function. Can be used in cases where no  known analytical distribution available. \n\nArguments\n\ndomain: domain specificatiom from DomainSets.jl package, by default the domain is set to DomainSets.FullSpace(). Use BayesBase.UnspecifiedDomain() to bypass domain checks.\nlogpdf: callable object that represents the logdensity. Can be un-normalised.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.ContinuousUnivariateLogPdf"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.ContinuousMultivariateLogPdf","page":"Home","text":"ContinuousMultivariateLogPdf{ D <: DomainSets.Domain, F } <: AbstractContinuousGenericLogPdf\n\nGeneric continuous multivariate distribution in a form of domain specification and logpdf function. Can be used in cases where no  known analytical distribution available. \n\nArguments\n\ndomain: multidimensional domain specification from DomainSets.jl package. Use BayesBase.UnspecifiedDomain() to bypass domain checks.\nlogpdf: callable object that accepts an AbstractVector as an input and represents the logdensity. Can be un-normalised.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.ContinuousMultivariateLogPdf"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.DiscreteUnivariateLogPdf","page":"Home","text":"DiscreteUnivariateLogPdf{ D <: DomainSets.Domain, F } <: AbstractDiscreteGenericLogPdf\n\nGeneric discrete univariate distribution in a form of domain specification and logpdf function. Can be used in cases where no  known analytical distribution available. \n\nArguments\n\ndomain: domain specificatiom from DomainSets.jl package, by default the domain is set to DomainSets.Integers(). Use BayesBase.UnspecifiedDomain() to bypass domain checks.\nlogpdf: callable object that represents the logdensity. Can be un-normalised.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.DiscreteUnivariateLogPdf"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.SampleList","page":"Home","text":"SampleList\n\nGeneric distribution represented as a list of weighted samples.\n\nArguments\n\nsamples::S\nweights::W: optional, equivalent to fill(1 / N, N) by default, where N is the length of samples container\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.SampleList"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.FactorizedJoint","page":"Home","text":"FactorizedJoint(components)\n\nFactorizedJoint represents a joint distribution of independent random variables.  Use component() function or square-brackets indexing to access the marginal distribution for individual variables. Use components() function to get a tuple of multipliers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.FactorizedJoint"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.MixtureDistribution","page":"Home","text":"MixtureDistribution(components, weights)\n\nA custom mixture distribution implementation, parameterized by:\n\nC type family of the mixture\nCT the type for the weights\n\nThis implementation solves:\n\nDistributions.jl Issue 1669\nReactiveMP.jl Issue 253\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.MixtureDistribution"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.Contingency","page":"Home","text":"Contingency(P, renormalize = Val(true))\n\nThe contingency distribution is a multivariate generalization of the categorical distribution. As a bivariate distribution, the  contingency distribution defines the joint probability over two unit vectors v1 and v2. The parameter P encodes a contingency matrix that specifies the probability of co-occurrence.\n\nv1 ∈ {0, 1}^d1 where Σ_j v1_j = 1\nv2 ∈ {0, 1}^d2 where Σ_k v2_k = 1\n\nP ∈ [0, 1]^{d1 × d2}, where Σ_jk P_jk = 1\n\nf(v1, v2, P) = Contingency(out1, out2 | P) = Π_jk P_jk^{v1_j * v2_k}\n\nA Contingency distribution over more than two variables requires higher-order tensors as parameters; these are not implemented in ReactiveMP.\n\nArguments:\n\nP, required, contingency matrix\nrenormalize, optional, supports either Val(true) or Val(false), specifies whether matrix P must be automatically renormalized. Does not modify the original P and allocates a new one for the renormalized version. If set to false the contingency matrix P must be normalized by hand, otherwise the result of related calculations might be wrong\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.Contingency"},{"category":"method","location":"#Base.prod-Tuple{BayesBase.UnspecifiedProd, Any, Any}","page":"Home","text":"prod(strategy, left, right)\n\nprod function is used to find a product of two probability distributions (or any other objects) over same variable (e.g. 𝓝(x|μ1, σ1) × 𝓝(x|μ2, σ2)). There are multiple strategies for prod function, e.g. ClosedProd, GenericProd or PreserveTypeProd.\n\nSee also: default_prod_rule, ClosedProd, PreserveTypeProd, GenericProd\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.prod"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.default_prod_rule","page":"Home","text":"default_prod_rule(::Type, ::Type)\n\nReturns the most suitable prod rule for two given distribution types. Returns UnspecifiedProd by default.\n\nSee also: prod, ClosedProd, GenericProd\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.default_prod_rule"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.UnspecifiedProd","page":"Home","text":"UnspecifiedProd\n\nA strategy for the prod function, which does not compute the prod, but instead fails in run-time and prints a descriptive error message.\n\nSee also: prod, ClosedProd, GenericProd\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.UnspecifiedProd"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.ClosedProd","page":"Home","text":"ClosedProd\n\nClosedProd is one of the strategies for prod function. For example, if both inputs are of type Distribution, then ClosedProd would fallback to PreserveTypeProd(Distribution).\n\nSee also: prod, PreserveTypeProd, GenericProd\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.ClosedProd"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.PreserveTypeProd","page":"Home","text":"PreserveTypeProd{T}\n\nPreserveTypeProd is one of the strategies for prod function. This strategy constraint an output of a prod to be in some specific form. By default it uses the strategy from default_prod_rule and converts the output to the prespecified type but can be overwritten  for some distributions for better performance.\n\nSee also: prod, ClosedProd, PreserveTypeLeftProd, PreserveTypeRightProd, GenericProd\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.PreserveTypeProd"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.PreserveTypeLeftProd","page":"Home","text":"PreserveTypeLeftProd\n\nAn alias for the PreserveTypeProd(L) where L is the type of the left argument of the prod function.\n\nSee also: prod, PreserveTypeProd, PreserveTypeRightProd, GenericProd\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.PreserveTypeLeftProd"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.PreserveTypeRightProd","page":"Home","text":"PreserveTypeRightProd\n\nAn alias for the PreserveTypeProd(R) where R is the type of the right argument of the prod function.    \n\nSee also: prod, PreserveTypeProd, PreserveTypeLeftProd, GenericProd\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.PreserveTypeRightProd"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.GenericProd","page":"Home","text":"GenericProd\n\nGenericProd is one of the strategies for prod function. This strategy does always produces a result,  even if the closed form product is not availble, in which case simply returns the ProductOf object. GenericProd sometimes  fallbacks to the default_prod_rule which it may or may not use under some circumstances.  For example if the default_prod_rule is ClosedProd - GenericProd will try to optimize the tree with  analytical closed solutions (if possible).\n\nSee also: prod, ProductOf, ClosedProd, PreserveTypeProd, default_prod_rule\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.GenericProd"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.ProductOf","page":"Home","text":"ProductOf\n\nA generic structure representing a product of two distributions.  Can be viewed as a tuple of (left, right).  Does not check nor supports neither variate forms during the creation stage. Uses the fuse_support function to fuse supports of two different distributions.\n\nThis object does not define any statistical properties (such as mean or var etc) and cannot be used as a distribution explicitly. Instead, it must be further approximated as a member of some other distribution. \n\nSee also: prod, GenericProd, fuse_supports\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.ProductOf"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.LinearizedProductOf","page":"Home","text":"LinearizedProductOf\n\nAn efficient linearized implementation of product of multiple distributions. This structure prevents ProductOf tree from growing too much in case of identical objects.  This trick significantly reduces Julia compilation times when closed product rules are not available but distributions are of the same type. Essentially this structure linearizes leaves of the ProductOf tree in case if it sees objects of the same type (via dispatch).\n\nSee also: ProductOf, [GenericProd]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.LinearizedProductOf"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.TerminalProdArgument","page":"Home","text":"TerminalProdArgument(argument)\n\nTerminalProdArgument is a specialized wrapper structure. When used as an argument to the prod function, it returns itself without considering any product strategy  and does not perform any safety checks (e.g. variate_form or support). Attempting to calculate the product of two instances of TerminalProdArgument will raise an error.  Use .argument field to get the underlying wrapped argument.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.TerminalProdArgument"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.resolve_prod_strategy","page":"Home","text":"resolve_prod_strategy(left, right)\n\nGiven two strategies, this function returns the one with higher priority, if possible.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.resolve_prod_strategy"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.deep_eltype","page":"Home","text":"deep_eltype(T)\n\nReturns:\n\ndeep_eltype of T if T is an AbstractArray container\nT otherwise\n\njulia> deep_eltype(Float64)\nFloat64\n\njulia> deep_eltype(Vector{Float64})\nFloat64\n\njulia> deep_eltype(Vector{Matrix{Vector{Float64}}})\nFloat64\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.deep_eltype"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.isequal_typeof","page":"Home","text":"isequal_typeof(left, right)\n\nAlias for typeof(left) === typeof(right), but can be specialized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.isequal_typeof"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.paramfloattype","page":"Home","text":"paramfloattype(distribution)\n\nReturns the underlying float type of distribution's parameters.\n\nSee also: promote_paramfloattype, convert_paramfloattype\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.paramfloattype"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.sampletype","page":"Home","text":"sampletype(distribution)\n\nReturns a type of the distribution. By default fallbacks to the eltype.\n\nSee also: samplefloattype, promote_sampletype, promote_samplefloattype\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.sampletype"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.samplefloattype","page":"Home","text":"samplefloattype(distribution)\n\nReturns a type of the distribution or the underlying float type in case if sample is Multivariate or Matrixvariate.  By default fallbacks to the deep_eltype(sampletype(distribution)).\n\nSee also: sampletype, promote_sampletype, promote_samplefloattype\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.samplefloattype"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.promote_variate_type","page":"Home","text":"promote_variate_type(::Type{ <: VariateForm }, distribution_type)\n\nPromotes (if possible) a distribution_type to be of the specified variate form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.promote_variate_type"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.promote_paramfloattype","page":"Home","text":"promote_paramfloattype(distributions...)\n\nPromotes paramfloattype of the distributions to a single type. See also promote_type.\n\nSee also: paramfloattype, convert_paramfloattype\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.promote_paramfloattype"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.promote_sampletype","page":"Home","text":"promote_sampletype(distributions...)\n\nPromotes sampletype of the distributions to a single type. See also promote_type.\n\nSee also: sampletype, samplefloattype, promote_samplefloattype\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.promote_sampletype"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.promote_samplefloattype","page":"Home","text":"promote_samplefloattype(distributions...)\n\nPromotes samplefloattype of the distributions to a single type. See also promote_type.\n\nSee also: sampletype, samplefloattype, promote_sampletype\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.promote_samplefloattype"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.convert_paramfloattype","page":"Home","text":"convert_paramfloattype(::Type{T}, distribution)\n\nConverts (if possible) the params float type of the distribution to be of type T.\n\nSee also: paramfloattype, promote_paramfloattype\n\n\n\n\n\nconvert_paramfloattype(::Type{T}, container)\n\nConverts (if possible) the elements of the container to be of type T.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.convert_paramfloattype"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.mirrorlog","page":"Home","text":"mirrorlog(x)\n\nReturns log(1 - x).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.mirrorlog"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.xtlog","page":"Home","text":"xtlog(x)\n\nReturns x * log(x).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.xtlog"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.logmvbeta","page":"Home","text":"logmvbeta(x)\n\nUses the numerically stable algorithm to compute the logarithm of the multivariate beta distribution over with the parameter vector x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.logmvbeta"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.clamplog","page":"Home","text":"clamplog(x)\n\nSame as log but clamps the input argument x to be in the range tiny <= x <= typemax(x) such that log(0) does not explode.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.clamplog"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.mvtrigamma","page":"Home","text":"mvtrigamma(p, x)\n\nComputes multivariate trigamma function .\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.mvtrigamma"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.dtanh","page":"Home","text":"dtanh(x)\n\nAlias for 1 - tanh(x) ^ 2\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.dtanh"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.probvec","page":"Home","text":"probvec(d)\n\nReturns the probability vector of the given distribution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.probvec"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.mcov!","page":"Home","text":"mcov!(Z, X::AbstractMatrix, Y::AbstractMatrix; tmp1 = zeros(eltype(Z), size(X, 2)), tmp2 = zeros(eltype(Z), size(Y, 2)), tmp3 = similar(X), tmp4 = similar(Y))\n\nSame as Statistics.cov(X, Y), but does not allocate the result. Instead uses a buffer Z to store the result in. Additionally, it allows for passing temporary buffers tmp1, tmp2, tmp3, tmp4 to avoid any allocations. Always computes corrected = true covariance matrix.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.mcov!"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.mean_std","page":"Home","text":"Alias for (mean(d), std(d)), but can be specialized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.mean_std"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.mean_var","page":"Home","text":"Alias for (mean(d), var(d)), but can be specialized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.mean_var"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.mean_cov","page":"Home","text":"Alias for (mean(d), cov(d)), but can be specialized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.mean_cov"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.mean_invcov","page":"Home","text":"Alias for (mean(d), invcov(d)), but can be specialized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.mean_invcov"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.mean_precision","page":"Home","text":"Alias for mean_invcov(d), but can be specialized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.mean_precision"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.weightedmean","page":"Home","text":"weightedmean(d)\n\nReturns the weighted mean of the given distribution.  Alias to invcov(d) * mean(d), but can be specialized\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.weightedmean"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.weightedmean_std","page":"Home","text":"Alias for (weightedmean(d), std(d)), but can be specialized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.weightedmean_std"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.weightedmean_var","page":"Home","text":"Alias for (weightedmean(d), var(d)), but can be specialized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.weightedmean_var"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.weightedmean_cov","page":"Home","text":"Alias for (weightedmean(d), cov(d)), but can be specialized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.weightedmean_cov"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.weightedmean_invcov","page":"Home","text":"Alias for weightedmean_invcov(d), but can be specialized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.weightedmean_invcov"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.ArrowheadMatrix","page":"Home","text":"ArrowheadMatrix{O, T, Z, P} <: AbstractMatrix{O}\n\nA structure representing an arrowhead matrix, which is a special type of sparse matrix.\n\nFields\n\nα::T: The scalar value at the bottom-right corner of the matrix.\nz::Z: A vector representing the last row/column (excluding the corner element).\nD::P: A vector representing the diagonal elements (excluding the corner element).\n\nConstructors\n\nArrowheadMatrix(a::T, z::Z, d::D) where {T,Z,D}\n\nConstructs an ArrowheadMatrix with the given α, z, and D values. The output type O is automatically determined as the promoted type of all input elements.\n\nOperations\n\nMatrix-vector multiplication: A * x or mul!(y, A, x)\nLinear system solving: A \\ b or ldiv!(x, A, b)\nConversion to dense matrix: convert(Matrix, A)\nInversion: inv(A) (returns an InvArrowheadMatrix)\n\nExamples\n\nα = 2.0\nz = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]\nD = [4.0, 5.0, 6.0]\nA = ArrowheadMatrix(α, z, D)\n\n# Matrix-vector multiplication\nx = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0]\ny = A * x\n\n# Solving linear system\nb = [7.0, 8.0, 9.0, 10.0]\nx = A \\ b\n\n# Convert to dense matrix\ndense_A = convert(Matrix, A)\n\nNotes\n\nThe matrix is singular if α - dot(z ./ D, z) = 0 or if any element of D is zero.\nFor best performance, use ldiv! for solving linear systems when possible.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.ArrowheadMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.InvArrowheadMatrix","page":"Home","text":"InvArrowheadMatrix{O, T, Z, P} <: AbstractMatrix{O}\n\nA wrapper structure representing the inverse of an ArrowheadMatrix.\n\nThis structure doesn't explicitly compute or store the inverse matrix. Instead, it stores a reference to the original ArrowheadMatrix and implements efficient operations that leverage the special structure of the arrowhead matrix.\n\nFields\n\nA::ArrowheadMatrix{O, T, Z, P}: The original ArrowheadMatrix being inverted.\n\nConstructors\n\nInvArrowheadMatrix(A::ArrowheadMatrix{O, T, Z, P})\n\nConstructs an InvArrowheadMatrix by wrapping the given ArrowheadMatrix.\n\nOperations\n\nMatrix-vector multiplication: A_inv * x or mul!(y, A_inv, x) (Equivalent to solving the system A * y = x)\nLinear system solving: A_inv \\ x (Equivalent to multiplication by the original matrix: A * x)\nConversion to dense matrix: convert(Matrix, A_inv) (Computes and returns the actual inverse as a dense matrix)\n\nExamples\n\nα = 2.0\nz = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]\nD = [4.0, 5.0, 6.0]\nA = ArrowheadMatrix(α, z, D)\nA_inv = inv(A)  # Returns an InvArrowheadMatrix\n\n# Multiplication (equivalent to solving A * y = x)\nx = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0]\ny = A_inv * x\n\n# Division (equivalent to multiplying by A)\nb = [5.0, 6.0, 7.0, 8.0]\nx = A_inv \\ b\n\n# Convert to dense inverse matrix\ndense_inv_A = convert(Matrix, A_inv)\n\nNotes\n\nThe inverse exists only if the original ArrowheadMatrix is non-singular.\nOperations with InvArrowheadMatrix do not explicitly compute the inverse, but instead solve the corresponding system with the original matrix.\n\nSee Also\n\nArrowheadMatrix: The original arrowhead matrix structure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.InvArrowheadMatrix"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.MatrixWithCachedInverse","page":"Home","text":"MatrixWithCachedInverse(A::AbstractMatrix)\n\nAn AbstractMatrix wrapper around an owned copy of a dense, real, symmetric positive-definite matrix that lazily caches its Cholesky factor and its inverse.\n\nConstruction copies A (a Symmetric or Hermitian view is materialized into a dense matrix), checks that it is square and symmetric, and performs no factorization. The first cholesky(A), isposdef(A), A \\ b or logdet(A) computes and caches the Cholesky factor. The first inv(A) computes the inverse once and returns another MatrixWithCachedInverse that already links back, so inv(A) === inv(A) and inv(inv(A)) === A hold without any recomputation. Positive definiteness is checked on the first factorization request (PosDefException, or false from isposdef). Symmetry and, for real elements, Hermitian symmetry are known without scanning.\n\nElement writes through the wrapper and its inverse are rejected; Matrix(A) returns an independent writable dense copy. copy(A) preserves the wrapper with independent storage and a fresh lock, reuses any cached Cholesky factor, and starts with an empty inverse cache. AbstractMatrix{T}(A) returns a new wrapper with empty caches, while convert(AbstractMatrix{T}, A) returns A itself when the element type already matches. Lazy initialization is synchronized with a lock shared by the pair, so concurrent first requests share one result.\n\nparent(A) exposes the owned mutable matrix without copying. Do not mutate it while using the wrapper: mutations can leave cached factors and inverses stale and break symmetry assumptions. There is no automatic cache invalidation. The .storage field is internal, not public API. Use Matrix(A) when an independent writable matrix is needed.\n\nFastCholesky\n\nWith FastCholesky loaded, fastcholesky, cholinv, chollogdet and cholinv_logdet reuse the same caches.\n\nReactant\n\nWith Reactant loaded (tested with Reactant 0.2.298 on Julia 1.13, CPU backend, Float32 and Float64), a wrapper converts with Reactant.to_rarray in any cache state, including a populated reciprocal pair, and can be constructed inside a compiled function. Inside compiled code inv, \\, logdet, * and broadcasting are supported with the following contract:\n\nCaches are never written during tracing. Host-populated caches become inputs of the compiled program (no factorization is emitted for them); empty caches are computed from the storage in the program on every execution.\nThe cache state is part of the compile contract: a function compiled on an empty-cache wrapper also accepts populated wrappers, but a function compiled on a populated wrapper fails on an empty one.\nJulia object identity and the host lock do not carry across the compiled boundary: a compiled inv returns a new wrapper whose inverse holds the input values but is not === the input.\nSymmetry and positive definiteness are not checked on traced input.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.MatrixWithCachedInverse"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.vague","page":"Home","text":"vague(distribution_type, [ dims... ])\n\nReturns uninformative probability distribution of the given type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.vague"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.logpdf_sampling_optimized","page":"Home","text":"logpdf_sampling_optimized(d)\n\nlogpdf_sample_optimized function takes as an input a distribution d and returns corresponding optimized two versions  for taking logpdf() and sampling with rand! respectively. Alias for (logpdf_optimized(d), sampling_optimized(d)), but can be specialized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.logpdf_sampling_optimized"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.logpdf_optimized","page":"Home","text":"logpdf_optimized(d)\n\nReturns a version of d specifically optimized to call logpdf(d, x). By default returns the same d, but can be specialized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.logpdf_optimized"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.sampling_optimized","page":"Home","text":"sampling_optimized(d)\n\nReturns a version of d specifically optimized to call rand and rand!. By default returns the same d, but can be specialized.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.sampling_optimized"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.fuse_supports","page":"Home","text":"fuse_supports(left, right)\n\nFuses supports left and right. By default, checks that the inputs are identical and throws an error otherwise. Can implement specific fusions for specific supports.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.fuse_supports"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.UnspecifiedDomain","page":"Home","text":"Unknown domain that is used as a placeholder when exact domain knowledge is unavailable\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.UnspecifiedDomain"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.UnspecifiedDimension","page":"Home","text":"Unknown dimension is equal and not equal to any number\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.UnspecifiedDimension"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.distribution_typewrapper","page":"Home","text":"Strips type parameters from the type of the distribution.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.distribution_typewrapper"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.CountingReal","page":"Home","text":"CountingReal\n\nCountingReal implements a real \"number\" that counts 'infinities' in a separate field. See also BayesBase.Infinity and BayesBase.MinusInfinity.\n\nArguments\n\nvalue::T: value of type <: Real\ninfinities::Int: number of added/subtracted infinities\n\njulia> r = BayesBase.CountingReal(0.0, 0)\nCountingReal{Float64}(0.0, 0)\n\njulia> float(r)\n0.0\n\njulia> r = r + BayesBase.Infinity(Float64)\nCountingReal{Float64}(0.0, 1)\n\njulia> float(r)\nInf\n\njulia> r = r + BayesBase.MinusInfinity(Float64)\nCountingReal{Float64}(0.0, 0)\n\njulia> float(r)\n0.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.CountingReal"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.Infinity","page":"Home","text":"An object representing infinity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.Infinity"},{"category":"function","location":"#BayesBase.MinusInfinity","page":"Home","text":"An object representing minus infinity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.MinusInfinity"},{"category":"type","location":"#BayesBase.InplaceLogpdf","page":"Home","text":"InplaceLogpdf(logpdf!)\n\nWraps a logpdf! function in a type that can later on be used for dispatch.  The sole purpose of this wrapper type is to allow for in-place logpdf operation on a batch of samples. Accepts a function logpdf! that takes two arguments: out and sample and writes the logpdf of the sample to the out array. A regular logpdf function can be converted to logpdf! by using convert(InplaceLogpdf, logpdf).\n\njulia> using Distributions, BayesBase\n\njulia> d = Beta(2, 3);\n\njulia> inplace = convert(BayesBase.InplaceLogpdf, (sample) -> logpdf(d, sample));\n\njulia> out = zeros(9);\n\njulia> inplace(out, 0.1:0.1:0.9)\n9-element Vector{Float64}:\n -0.028399474521697776\n  0.42918163472548043\n  0.5675839575845996\n  0.5469646703818638\n  0.4054651081081646\n  0.14149956227369964\n -0.2797139028026039\n -0.9571127263944104\n -2.2256240518579173\n\njulia> using Distributions, BayesBase\n\njulia> d = Beta(2, 3);\n\njulia> inplace = BayesBase.InplaceLogpdf((out, sample) -> out .= logpdf.(d, sample));\n\njulia> out = zeros(9);\n\njulia> inplace(out, 0.1:0.1:0.9)\n9-element Vector{Float64}:\n -0.028399474521697776\n  0.42918163472548043\n  0.5675839575845996\n  0.5469646703818638\n  0.4054651081081646\n  0.14149956227369964\n -0.2797139028026039\n -0.9571127263944104\n -2.2256240518579173\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"BayesBase.InplaceLogpdf"}]
}
